{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789314266,"nonce":"8gUCt1SDVATxwZiPBbXLBh00sZE=","title":"The LLVM loop of bench_closure_call re-checks a loop-invariant closure every iteration","message":"NUC: the closure_call re-tier loop runs ~5 cycles per iteration against\n~3 for free_call's, and hybrid stays at 117-124ms where the JVM is 102 and\nAOT 117. The guarded devirt fires (`devirt closure fast-arm 3`) and the hit\narm has `step` inlined, but the guard's inputs are re-derived per\niteration: `f` is null-checked, `hasValue` loaded and tested for the\nwrapper shape, `fun` and `value` loaded (all `!invariant.load`), then\n`fun == sentinel` and `hasValue == 0` compared -- ~12 uops and 3 loads\nthat LICM cannot hoist because they sit under the in-loop null check, and\nthe null branch is not trivially unswitchable (it is not at the header).\n`default\u003cO3\u003e` did not help (closure_call +3%, method_call -5%; the\nnon-trivial unswitch's cost model refuses the body with its bridge paths).\nThe loop also keeps the trip bound in a stack slot (`cmpl %r13d, -80(%rbp)`).\n\nOptions: hoist a loop-invariant closure's null check and header loads in\nAIR (a NullCheck on a value defined outside the loop, at a header that is\nreached on the first iteration, can move to the preheader; for an OSR\nentry the frame is already inside the loop), so the guard becomes one\ncompare on a hoisted value; or mark the OSR-restored closure pointer\n`nonnull dereferenceable(32)` when the AIR type says it is a closure and\nthe null check has been hoisted, which lets LICM speculate the loads.","files":null}]}